3. THE ITAR WALL AND THE REAL DOOR THROUGH IT

3.1 The Legal Test, Correctly Sourced
Under ITAR, the U.S. Munitions List (USML) Category IV covers launch vehicles and their specially designed parts. A part becomes controlled because of its design relationship to a defense article, not its complexity.

22 CFR § 120.41(b) (formerly § 121.1 prior to the 2024 Export Control Reform) provides explicit textual exclusions. A part is not “specially designed” if it is:

· A fastener (screw, bolt, nut, rivet, pin, etc.), washer, spacer, insulator, grommet, bushing, spring, wire, or solder—regardless of form;
· Or if it has the same function, performance, and form/fit as an article already in production for a non‑USML application.

These are black‑letter carve‑outs. Standard fasteners, generic brackets sold into multiple industries, and off‑the‑shelf hardware are categorically outside ITAR by the text of the law. Appendix B reproduces the exact regulatory language.

3.2 Corridor 1: Ground Support Equipment — Generic vs. Space‑Grade
Not all GSE is created equal. A distinction is essential to avoid accidental ITAR contamination.

GSE Type Definition ITAR Risk Example
Generic GSE Built from customer‑provided outer dimensions, mass, and load requirements only. No ITAR‑controlled interface drawings. Low. Strong candidate for § 120.41(b) exclusion. Transport stand for a cylindrical section, designed in‑house to performance specs.
Space‑Grade GSE Requires integration with flight‑article interfaces, or is built from an ITAR‑controlled drawing (e.g., a SpaceX engine‑handling fixture design). High. The drawing or the interface data itself is a defense article. A CJ determination or DSP‑5 license is mandatory. Engine integration dolly designed from an ITAR‑marked CAD model.

Only generic GSE is safe for Phase 1 without a license. The contract must carefully circumscribe what technical data is transmitted. A detailed decision tree is provided in Appendix A.

3.3 Corridor 2: The Indirect Path — EAR Only Until USML‑to‑CCL Is Final
The original roadmap assumed that a Moroccan shop could receive USML hardware from a U.S. Tier‑1, perform work, and return it with only redacted data. This is legally incorrect: the physical part is a defense article, and its export requires a Department of State license (DSP‑5) regardless of what data accompanies it.

The correct, legally sound alternative is to limit the indirect path to Commerce Control List (ECCN 9A515) or EAR99 items. Many structural components for launch vehicles may already be EAR‑controlled or commercial. The U.S. intermediary ships these items under existing license exceptions (e.g., STA) or no license, and the Moroccan shop returns them after processing. No ITAR risk attaches.

If a partner wishes to send a USML part, the U.S. entity must first obtain a DSP‑5 temporary export license. This is feasible but adds lead time. The roadmap therefore prioritizes the EAR corridor until the USML‑to‑CCL reclassification creates broader access.

3.4 Regulatory Direction of Travel
In late 2024, BIS published an interim final rule proposing to move certain space‑related articles from USML to CCL. The public comment period closed on 22 November 2024. As of July 2026, no final rule has been published. The roadmap does not depend on it; it merely monitors the development and can accelerate Phase 2 if and when the rule is finalized.

3.5 The Formal Path: Commodity Jurisdiction
Where classification is uncertain, a CJ request is the mandatory step. Note: only a U.S. person or a foreign government may file Form DS‑4076. A private Moroccan company cannot self‑file. The Moroccan cluster will either engage U.S. legal counsel or rely on a U.S. partner to file. A redacted example CJ filing for a generic GSE transport cradle is attached as Appendix A, showing the exact logic trail an applicant would use.

