A.4 Legal Logic Trail

The CJ request must demonstrate, with contemporaneous documentation, that the article satisfies at least one exclusion in § 120.41(b). For this cradle, the primary argument is § 120.41(b)(3):

> A part … is not specially designed if it has the same function, performance capabilities, and the same or equivalent form and fit as a commodity … used in or with a commodity that (i) is or was in production, and (ii) is not enumerated on the USML. 

The filer must attach evidence that:
1. The cradle’s function (static ground support during transport) is identical to commercial cradles;
2. Its performance capabilities (load rating, adjustability) match or are exceeded by commercial equivalents;
3. Its form and fit (circular base, vertical columns, load pads at specified spacing) are equivalent to cradles already in production for non‑USML applications.

If DDTC concurs, the response letter will state that the article is not subject to the ITAR. This is not an export license; it is a binding jurisdictional determination that protects all subsequent deliveries of identical cradles, provided no design change alters the basis for the exclusion.

