§ 120.41(b)(4)	Developed for use with both USML and non‑USML commodities	Requires contemporaneous development documents showing dual‑use intent from inception. Difficult to invoke retroactively.	
§ 120.41(b)(5)	General‑purpose commodity with no knowledge of specific end use	Requires marketing plans, patent declarations, or contracts proving generic intent at the time of development.	

---

B.4 Relationship to USML Category IV

USML Category IV(h) controls “All specifically designed or modified components, parts, accessories, attachments, and associated equipment for the articles in this category” — i.e., launch vehicles, guided missiles, and rockets.  The § 120.41(b) exclusions are the statutory door through which an article otherwise described by Category IV(h) can escape ITAR control. The burden of proof rests on the applicant to demonstrate that the exclusion applies.

---

B.5 Relationship to the EAR and ECCN 9A515

Articles excluded from the USML by operation of § 120.41(b)(1)–(3) generally fall under the Export Administration Regulations (EAR). Space‑related items are classified primarily under ECCN 9A515 (spacecraft and related articles) and ECCN 9A004 (ISS and JWST items). 

In 2024, the Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) issued an interim final rule that:
- Moved numerous spacecraft components from 9A515.x to 9A515.y, reducing license requirements for allied countries;
- Created new ECCNs for ion thrusters (9A515.i), control‑moment gyroscopes (9A515.j), and satellite release mechanisms (9A515.k);
- Added license exception CSA (Commercial Space Activities) for certain space tourism and research activities. 

As of July 2026, no final rule has completed the full USML‑to‑CCL reclassification for launch‑vehicle structural components. The Moroccan roadmap therefore treats the EAR corridor (ECCN 9A515 / EAR99) as the legally sound intermediate path, while monitoring the regulatory trajectory.

